    <Section n="00" title="The chips are<br>in the building." acid id="brief">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p>At the end of October 2025 the chief executive of Microsoft said the quiet part on a podcast. "The biggest issue we are now having is not a compute glut, but it's power," Satya Nadella said. If the buildings could not be finished fast enough close to power, "you may actually have a bunch of chips sitting in inventory that I can't plug in. In fact, that is my problem today." <span class="cite">{c(92, 91)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> His chief financial officer, Amy Hood, told analysts that for the past few years what the company lacked was not chips but "the space or the power" to put them in, and, of capacity against demand, that it had been "short now for many quarters". <span class="cite">{c(93)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
        <p>One of the three most valuable companies in the world, in the largest build-out in history, bought the hardware and could not turn all of it on. <span class="cite">{c(94)}</span></p>
        <p class="beat">This edition is about why. Not the chips. Everything the chips wait for.</p>
        <p class="mono-b">00 · The brief</p>
        <p>It started with a summary that said America was stuck. Turbines come from China. Rare earths cannot be refined at home. Electricians are being bid away from housing. Banks are getting nervous. I checked every claim against primary sources. Some were wrong, and the ones that were wrong were the ones that would get the whole argument dismissed.</p>
        <p>What survived is a queue. Chips wait for a building. The building waits for a grid connection. The connection waits for a study, the study waits for a rule, and the power behind it waits for a turbine with a 2031 delivery slot. Each wait is rational on its own. Together they stall.</p>
        <p>Every material claim below carries one of three marks.</p>
      </div>
      <Rail items={[
        ['F', '<b>Verified fact.</b> Confirmed in a primary source or credible reporting, listed at the back.'],
        ['I', '<b>Sourced inference.</b> My reasoning from the facts. Mine to defend, yours to argue with.'],
        ['Q', '<b>Open question.</b> Unknown, disputed, or untested.'],
      ]} />
    </Section>

    <Section n="01" title="474 gigawatts<br>asking." ground="bottle" id="queue">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="mono-b">01 · The queue</p>
        <p>Texas runs its own grid. In July 2026 its operator, ERCOT, told a Texas Senate committee that about 474 gigawatts of large loads were asking to connect, roughly nine in ten of them data centres. <span class="cite">{c(95)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> The grid's record peak demand, set in July, is about 91 gigawatts. <span class="cite">{c(3)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> The requests add up to more than five Texases. <span class="cite">{c(2)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
        <p>Oncor, one Dallas utility, holds nearly 300 gigawatts of requests by itself. <span class="cite">{c(4)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> In September ERCOT provisionally sorted its first batch: 66 gigawatts conditionally included as base load and about 128 more as studied load, 302 gigawatts excluded; final decisions are due in December. <span class="cite">{c(5)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> On 3 August the governor ordered an audit of every data centre advancing through ERCOT's interconnection process before any more are approved to move forward; ERCOT then paused energisation approvals for data centres and crypto-mining facilities of 75 megawatts or more. <span class="cite">{c(96, 5)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
        <p>The rest of the country is slower. About 2,061 gigawatts of generation and storage sat in US interconnection queues at the end of 2025, and, in the regions with data, the median project built that year took more than five years from request to operation. <span class="cite">{c(6)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> In northern Virginia, Dominion said in 2024 that a large load could wait up to seven years. <span class="cite">{c(7, 104)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> PJM, the grid that serves Virginia, has now cleared its capacity auction short of its reliability requirement twice running, the first times in its history that the whole region has fallen short. <span class="cite">{c(8)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
        <p>A request is not a project. <span class="cite">{c(6)}</span> Many may be speculative, experts say, filed in several places at once to hold a place in line. <span class="cite">{c(2)}</span> That is part of the problem: studying the ghosts delays the real ones. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-b side">
        <figure class="field bars" aria-label="ERCOT large-load requests against grid size">
