      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="03" title="Without this,<br>nothing." ground="bottle" id="nothing">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="mono-b">03 · The chain</p>
        <p>Twenty-one pieces the build needs. Each one below shows what it needs, what it enables, how long it takes to replace from a standing start, and where the number comes from, and then what becomes impossible downstream without it.</p>
        <p>Read the transformer first. Then read EUV lithography, one of ten pieces with nothing upstream of it, and the only one of those that takes a decade to replace.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="c-full pieces" aria-label="The chain, twenty-one pieces">
        {chain.map((c) => (
          <li class="field piece" id={`piece-${c.id}`}>
            <p class="mono kind">{c.k}{c.yrs ? ` · ${c.yrs} yr to replace` : ''}</p>
            <h3 class="piece-t">{c.t}</h3>
            <dl class="rail">
              <dt>Status</dt><dd>{c.have}</dd>
              <dt>Cost and time</dt><dd>{c.cost}</dd>
              <dt>Without it</dt><dd>{c.without}</dd>
              <dt>Source</dt><dd>{c.src}</dd>
            </dl>
            <dl class="links">
              <dt class="mono">Needs</dt><dd>{names(c.needs).join(' · ')}</dd>
              <dt class="mono">Enables</dt><dd>{names(c.enables).join(' · ')}</dd>
            </dl>
            <p class="mono">Critical path from a standing start</p>
            <p class="num crit">{fmt(c.crit, 1)} yrs</p>
            <p class="gone"><b>If removed,</b> {c.gone.length ? `${numberWord(c.gone.length)} ${c.gone.length === 1 ? 'piece becomes' : 'pieces become'} impossible: ${names(c.gone).join(', ')}.` : 'nothing downstream depends on it.'}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </Section>

    <Section n="04" title="Thirty-five<br>dollars, or twenty." id="hands">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="mono-b">04 · The hands</p>
        <p>Somebody has to wire it.</p>
        <p>In Abilene, Texas, where the first Stargate campus is going up, a local electrical contractor who wires new homes says data-centre electricians earn about 35 dollars an hour plus overtime and per diem. He pays about 20, and has lost five workers to the data centres. <span class="cite">{c(19)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> An Abilene builder told the Texas Tribune his houses now take two months longer than before the data centres came. <span class="cite">{c(19)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
        <p>It shows in the national numbers. Spending on data-centre construction ran at an annualised 85 billion dollars in August 2026, up 73 percent in a year. New single-family construction spending was 3.5 percent lower than a year earlier, within the survey's margin of error. <span class="cite">{c(20)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Lennar's chief executive told investors in September that "immigration enforcement and enthusiastic data center construction continue to create tightness in certain geographies." <span class="cite">{c(21)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> KB Home said the same month it had not had a lot of issues getting labour to its sites. <span class="cite">{c(21)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Both can be true in different places.</p>
        <p>Electricians held about 821,000 jobs in the US in 2025, and nearly one in five electricians is 55 or older. <span class="cite">{c(22, 121)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> In 2024 immigrants were 26 percent of the construction workforce, a record, <span class="cite">{c(24)}</span> and a Brookings study in May estimated that enforcement surges left construction employment about 2 percent below its expected path in the 86 metros hit hardest, an estimate its authors' September update does not repeat. <span class="cite">{c(25, 122)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
        <p class="mono-b">04 · Where the answer is less dramatic</p>
        <p>A hyperscale site needs roughly one peak worker per megawatt. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> At that ratio, even the 66 gigawatts ERCOT conditionally included as base load would occupy a small share of American electrician jobs at any one time. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span> Labour is not what caps the data centres. It is where their cost lands: in wages a homebuilder cannot match, and in houses that take longer to finish. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span></p>
      </div>
      <Stats class="c-b" items={[
        ['$85B', 'Data-centre construction, annualised, Aug 2026. Up 73% in a year. Census.'],
        ['−3.5%', 'New single-family construction spending, against Aug 2025.'],
        ['821k', 'US electrician jobs, 2025. Nearly one in five 55 or older.'],
        ['+2 mo', 'Added to an Abilene builder\'s houses since the data centres came.'],
      ]} />
    </Section>

    <Section n="05" title="Move the<br>numbers." ground="bottle" id="model">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="mono-b">05 · The model</p>
        <p>Each year, data-centre load asks for power. It can get it through a grid tie-in or by building its own plant. The grid path is capped by whichever is smallest that year: interconnection throughput, firm generation free for new load, or transformer deliveries. Both paths draw on the same electricians. Chips keep arriving regardless.</p>
        <p>Some defaults are set from the published figures above; the rest are my own choices. Every input carries a note, with the published figure nearest to it where there is one.</p>
        <p>At the defaults, transformers bind first, then the tie-in, then firm generation from 2029, as spare capacity dwindles before the new turbine slots open in 2030; in 2028 and 2030 the two are level. The queue never clears. Stranded compute peaks at about 34 gigawatts. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span> The 2028 backlog in the model, about 34 gigawatts, sits close to Morgan Stanley's reported estimate of a 32 gigawatt shortfall through 2028. <span class="cite">{c(27)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> The model's demand default cites the same estimate, so the two are not independent.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-full field scroll inputs" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The model's inputs">
        <table>
          <caption class="sr">The model's inputs, each with its published default and where the number comes from.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Input</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Where the number comes from</th></tr></thead>
          {C.map(([g, list]) => (
            <tbody>
              <tr class="grp"><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="3" class="mono-b">{g}</th></tr>
              {list.map((r) => (<tr><th scope="row">{r[1]}</th><td class="n">{show(r, r[5] as number)}</td><td>{r[7]}</td></tr>))}
            </tbody>
          ))}
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="c-full field run">
        <p class="mono run-h">The model at those defaults, 2026 to 2032</p>
        <Stats class="readout" items={readout} />
        <div class="binds">
          <p class="mono">Binds on</p>
          <ol>{R.map((r) => (<li><span class="mono yr">{r.y}</span><span class="st">{NM[r.bind]}</span></li>))}</ol>
        </div>
        <figure class="bars flow" aria-label="Load asking for power against load energised, by year">
          <figcaption class="mono">Load asking for power against load energised · GW a year</figcaption>
          <p class="mono key"><span class="sw g"></span>Grid tie-in <span class="sw b"></span>On-site plant <span class="sw a"></span>Load asking, the rest carried as backlog</p>
          {R.map((r) => (
            <div class="bar">
              <span class="mono lbl">{r.y}</span>
              <span class="track" style={`width:${pct(r.want, askMax)}`}><span class="fill g" style={`width:${pct(r.g, r.want)}`}></span><span class="fill b" style={`width:${pct(r.b, r.want)}`}></span></span>
              <span class="num val">{fmt(r.tot)} of {fmt(r.want)}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </figure>
        <figure class="bars flow" aria-label="Chips delivered against chips powered, cumulative">
          <figcaption class="mono">Chips delivered against chips powered · cumulative GW</figcaption>
          <p class="mono key"><span class="sw g"></span>Powered <span class="sw a"></span>Delivered, the rest stranded</p>
          {R.map((r) => (
            <div class="bar">
              <span class="mono lbl">{r.y}</span>
              <span class="track" style={`width:${pct(r.cc, strMax)}`}><span class="fill g" style={`width:${pct(r.cp, r.cc)}`}></span></span>
              <span class="num val">{fmt(r.str)} stranded</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </figure>
        <div class="scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The model, year by year">
          <table>
            <caption class="sr">The model at its published defaults, year by year, in gigawatts.</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Asks</th><th scope="col">Tie-in cap</th><th scope="col">Firm cap</th><th scope="col">Transformer cap</th><th scope="col">Grid</th><th scope="col">On-site</th><th scope="col">Backlog</th><th scope="col">Stranded</th><th scope="col">Binds on</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {R.map((r) => (<tr><th scope="row">{r.y}</th><td class="n">{fmt(r.want)}</td><td class="n">{fmt(r.caps.grid)}</td><td class="n">{fmt(r.caps.gen)}</td><td class="n">{fmt(r.caps.xfmr)}</td><td class="n">{fmt(r.g)}</td><td class="n">{fmt(r.b)}</td><td class="n">{fmt(r.back)}</td><td class="n">{fmt(r.str)}</td><td class="n">{NM[r.bind]}</td></tr>))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="06" title="Build your<br>own plant." id="bypass">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="mono-b">06 · The bypass</p>
        <p>The fastest way around the queue is to leave it. Cleanview counts about 90 gigawatts of on-site generation announced at 59 US data centres, more than a quarter of planned data-centre capacity. About 2 gigawatts of it was running as of mid-2026. <span class="cite">{c(28)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
        <p>xAI's Memphis-area campuses ran about 1.5 gigawatts of gas turbines in mid-2026, Cleanview counts, a total that grew as xAI added dozens of temporary mobile turbines at Southaven, Mississippi. <span class="cite">{c(28)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Mississippi's regulator lets those run without a permit, reading federal rules as exempting temporary-mobile turbines for up to a year; environmental groups dispute that reading. <span class="cite">{c(125)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Under a settlement Louisiana's regulator approved in August 2025, Meta has agreed to cover the full cost of three new Entergy gas plants for fifteen years, though not of the transmission line. <span class="cite">{c(126, 127)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
        <p>This is the turbine queue again, in a smaller size. Aeroderivative and reciprocating units come from a different, faster factory pool than the large frames, which is why they are being bought. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span></p>
        <p class="mono-b">06 · Three grids, barely touching</p>
        <p>Texas keeps its own interconnection to stay outside federal transmission rules. <span class="cite">{c(98)}</span> Its generators reach connection agreements faster than the national average, and when it runs short, the help it can draw from its neighbours is small. <span class="cite">{c(97, 32)}</span> Its direct-current ties to the rest of the country total 820 megawatts, plus 400 to Mexico, about 1.3 percent of its peak. <span class="cite">{c(32, 3)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> A 3 gigawatt line to Mississippi, Southern Spirit, is scheduled for service somewhere between 2029 and 2032, depending on which of its own documents you read. <span class="cite">{c(101, 33)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
      </div>
      <Stats class="c-b" items={[
        ['90 GW', 'On-site generation announced at US data centres.'],
        ['~2 GW', 'Of it running. Cleanview, mid 2026.'],
        ['1.3%', 'Texas tie capacity as a share of its peak.'],
      ]} />
    </Section>

    <Section n="07" title="Negative<br>free cash flow." ground="bottle" id="money">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="mono-b">07 · The money</p>
        <p>The hyperscalers can afford this. Can't they?</p>
        <p>In the second quarter of 2026 Alphabet reported negative free cash flow: minus 5.9 billion dollars, on 44.9 billion of capital spending. <span class="cite">{c(128, 34)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Amazon's free cash flow over the trailing twelve months went to minus 7.6 billion. <span class="cite">{c(35)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Meta's second-quarter free cash flow was 784 million dollars on 31 billion of capital spending. <span class="cite">{c(36)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> In the June quarter, Microsoft is the only one of the four still clearly positive. <span class="cite">{c(129)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Bank of America reportedly projects the eight largest global hyperscalers move from 180 billion dollars of combined free cash flow in 2025 to minus 64 billion this year and minus 186 billion in 2028. <span class="cite">{c(37)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
        <p>These are still among the most profitable companies ever built. The profit comes from search, advertising, cloud and software. The spending goes to AI. What has not been shown is that the second pays back the first. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span> Bain estimates AI needs about 2 trillion dollars a year of revenue by 2030 to fund the build and, even counting AI savings, puts the annual shortfall at 800 billion. <span class="cite">{c(38)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> OpenAI reportedly expects to burn about 25 billion dollars this year. <span class="cite">{c(39)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
        <p>The rest is borrowed. Data-centre securitisations outstanding reached about 61 billion dollars in 2026, from 4 billion in 2020, by Barclays' count. <span class="cite">{c(40)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> That debt is written against compute revenue that starts when the power comes on. Every month the tie-in slips is a month of interest with nothing to pay it. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-b">
        <p class="mono">Eight largest global hyperscalers · combined free cash flow · 2028 · Bank of America, as reported</p>
        <p class="num figure fig-m">−$186B</p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="08" title="The other<br>side." id="china">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="mono-b">08 · Side by side</p>
        <p>Pairs, each row with one linked source; the row notes name a second source or a different year where a row has one, and n/a marks a row with no Chinese figure. Where China is behind, the row says so. Bars are proportional within each pair.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-full cmp">
        {compare.map(({ k, list }) => (
          <div class="scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label={`Side by side · ${k}`}>
            <table>
              <caption class="mono-b cmp-h">{k}</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">China</th><th scope="col">United States</th><th scope="col">Note and source</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {list.map(({ r, m, src }) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{r[0]}{r[3] && <span class="mono unit">{r[3]}</span>}</th>
                    <td class="v"><span class="num">{r[1]}</span><span class="track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="fill" style={`width:${pct(r[4] as number, m)}`}></span></span></td>
                    <td class="v"><span class="num">{r[2]}</span><span class="track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="fill us" style={`width:${pct(r[5] as number, m)}`}></span></span></td>
                    <td class="note">{r[6]} {src.n ? <span class="cite">[{src.n}]{r[8] ? ' ' + c(...(r[8] as number[])) : ''}</span> : <span class="mono url">{src.url}</span>}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="mono-b">08 · China's own queue</p>
        <p>The comparison is not a scoreboard. In March 2025 MIT Technology Review relayed reports by the local outlets Jiazi Guangnian and 36Kr that up to 80 percent of China's newly built computing resources remained unused. <span class="cite">{c(78)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Huawei shipped about 800,000 Ascend accelerators in 2025 against roughly 4.5 million from Nvidia, the most advanced node it can use is held at a 7 nanometre class without EUV, <span class="cite">{c(62, 132)}</span> and its stockpile of foreign high-bandwidth memory is expected to run out this year. <span class="cite">{c(62)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Listed solar makers' combined losses for 2025 were estimated in March at more than 50 billion yuan, and the car industry's average margin fell to a record low of 4.1 percent in 2025, then to 2.9 percent in January and February 2026. <span class="cite">{c(79, 80)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Youth unemployment was 18.9 percent in August. <span class="cite">{c(81)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span></p>
        <p>China builds faster and overbuilds. America plans more and builds less. Each is paying for its habit. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span></p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="09" title="Nine claims,<br>checked." ground="bottle" id="checked">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="mono-b">09 · What did not hold</p>
        <p>The claims that started this, and a few that travel with them, against the record.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-full scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Nine claims, checked">
        <table class="verdicts">
          <caption class="sr">Nine claims, each with its verdict and what the record shows.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Claim</th><th scope="col">Verdict</th><th scope="col">What the record shows</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {checked.map(([q, v, w, s]) => (<tr><th scope="row">{q}</th><td class="n">{v}</td><td>{w}{s && <span class="cite">{' ' + c(...(s as number[]))}</span>}</td></tr>))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="10" title="Magnets,<br>not megawatts." id="minerals">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="mono-b">10 · The other queue</p>
        <p>Rare earths and gallium are a real chokepoint, but they choke a different line. They gate permanent magnets, gallium-nitride power electronics and radar. <span class="cite">{c(69)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> A gas turbine uses rare earths too, in the coatings on its hottest parts. <span class="cite">{c(69)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> But the turbines and transformers that hold up the data centres wait mainly on steel, copper and factory slots. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span></p>
        <p>China refines 91 percent of the world's rare earths, 99 percent of its gallium and 94 percent of its germanium. <span class="cite">{c(69)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> The United States has produced no primary gallium since 1987. <span class="cite">{c(70)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Six of China's October 2025 export-control announcements are suspended until 10 November 2026, <span class="cite">{c(118, 86)}</span> and its US-only curbs on gallium and germanium until 27 November. <span class="cite">{c(119)}</span> Its April 2025 licensing of seven medium and heavy rare earths has no end date and was left out of the suspension. <span class="cite">{c(120, 118)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> Whether the suspensions are extended is the open question of the next six weeks. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.Q}>Q</span></p>
      </div>
      <dl class="c-b dates">
        <dt class="mono-b">2026</dt><dd>MP Materials expects to commission its heavy rare-earth plant at Mountain Pass; in May it said commissioning was "set to begin imminently". <span class="cite">{c(87, 151)}</span> Alcoa's Wagerup gallium plant breaks ground. <span class="cite">{c(150)}</span></dd>
        <dt class="mono-b">2027</dt><dd>Energy Fuels' terbium and dysprosium circuits at White Mesa, Utah, due by year end. It first made NdPr commercially in June 2024, up to 1,000 tonnes a year. <span class="cite">{c(153, 154)}</span> Rio Tinto's gallium pilot plant in Saguenay, Quebec, due to operate; a demonstration plant of up to 4 tonnes a year is planned beside it. <span class="cite">{c(155)}</span></dd>
        <dt class="mono-b">2028</dt><dd>MP's magnet plant in Northlake, Texas, begins commissioning; 7,000 tonnes a year once scaled. <span class="cite">{c(87, 152)}</span> First production APG-85 radars for the F-35. <span class="cite">{c(82)}</span></dd>
        <dt class="mono-b">2029</dt><dd>Korea Zinc's Tennessee smelter due for completion and commissioning; 54 tonnes of gallium and 44 of germanium a year planned from 2030. <span class="cite">{c(156, 89)}</span></dd>
        <dt class="mono-b">29 yrs</dt><dd>Average discovery to production for a US mine, if the 10 of 13 in S&amp;P Global's sample not yet producing start by 2030. <span class="cite">{c(90)}</span></dd>
      </dl>
    </Section>

    <Section n="11" title="The order<br>of things." ground="bottle" id="order">
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p class="mono-b">11 · What it would take</p>
        <p>Money is not the binding constraint. Between them, the four have guided over 700 billion dollars of capital spending for this year. <span class="cite">{c(34, 41, 129)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.F}>F</span> The constraints are things money buys slowly: a factory slot, a permit, a study, an apprentice who takes four or five years to qualify.</p>
        <p>In my view, three changes would move the dates. Queue rules that make speculative requests cost something, so the ghosts leave and the studies speed up. Transformer and turbine factory capacity committed now, since anything ordered today lands around 2030. And transmission permitting measured in years, not decades, because SunZia spent more than seventeen years in permits and approvals. <span class="cite">{c(42)}</span> <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span></p>
        <p>None of this is a technology problem. Every piece in the chain is a solved engineering problem with a waiting list. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span></p>
        <p class="beat mal">The chips are the fast part.</p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section n="12" title="Sources" id="sources">
      <div class="c-full">
        <p class="mono">Read in October 2026; checked 1 Nov 2026</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="c-full sources">
        {sources.map((s, i) => (<li><span class="mono-b n">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span><span>{s}</span></li>))}
      </ol>
      <div class="c-full">
        <p class="mono">Checked 1 Nov 2026: every source opened that would open; lines changed where a source said less; the rest are marked as reported or wait on the owner. The print is left as issued.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="c-a prose">
        <p>Several 2026 figures rest on a single source or on reporting that cites unnamed people, OpenAI's burn and Morgan Stanley's shortfall estimate in particular; they are reported, not established. ERCOT's July peak is preliminary. Input values marked as assumptions are mine. None of this is investment advice. <span class="mark" aria-label={MARK.I}>I</span></p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <EditionEnd slug={ed.slug}>
      The chips are<br>the fast part.
    </EditionEnd>
  </main>
</Base>

